  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently, storms are beginning to form in the Sky Islands\nacross Southeastern Arizona. Through this afternoon and evening\nstorms will move off the terrain into the valleys and move West\nto Northwest at 10-15 kts. Main impacts from storms today are\nprimarily strong outflow winds at 40-60 mph that can loft areas\nof blowing dust and reduce visibilities. With this modest storm\nmotion, flash flooding will be isolated and mainly limited to\nbackbuilding/training thunderstorms across the area. \n\nLooking at tomorrow, it will all depend on how strong and\nwidespread the thunderstorm activity we see today. Some CAMs\nlike the HRRR is more ambitious with scattered thunderstorms\nacross much of Southeastern Arizona, but if the area is worked\nover enough from the activity today, that will keep much of the\nstronger storm activity at a minimum. If there are storms,\nhazards will be similar to today with the stronger wind gusts,\nthen flash flooding.\n\nThis weekend continues to look more active, but still unclear\nwhich day will be more impactful out of the two days. As the\narea of high pressure begins to move east and trough digs down\nto our west, this synoptic forcing will allow for longer lived\nstorms. As the upper level flow shifts SW, ample moisture will \ncome into the area, increasing PWs to 1.5 inches in Tucson by\nSaturday. This increase in moisture will switch the primary\nhazard to flash flooding. \n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday with\nthe hottest temperatures through Friday. Temperatures will lower\nto near normal levels Sunday into next week, with perhaps Monday\neven coming in slightly below normal.",
